out of everything that we love about  souls you know the law the characters  the combat I think that PvP is the thing  that they need to get right the most in  my opinion it's a vitally important part  of anyone's first playthrough and I  think that the game with the best pvp  system will be the one that everyone  plays even years after its release so  over the next 12 minutes I'm going to  run you through everything that the  network test has taught us about Dark  Souls 3s PvP  let's see where things are headed first  up let's talk about how you invade you  invade using the red eye orb item  similar to in Dark Souls 1 this item  lets you invade as many times as you  want Miyazaki has stated that players  will no longer be locked behind  consumable invasion items which is good  for encouraging invasions additionally  players now matched on soul level not  soul memory and finally I had really  fast invasions during the test so  everything's looking good on that front  everyone's biggest concern in the  network test was that you could only be  matched with hosts who had summoned help  meaning that most of your fights were  against multiple players most fights  involved teaming up with a red phantom  for victory  like you see here or it meant overcoming  multiple opponents all by yourselves  most of your fights were 1 versus 2 or 1  versus 3 or even 1 versus 4 which is  pretty brutal but the thing to remember  is that this was probably intentional  from software probably wanted to fill  these worlds with as many phantoms as  possible this is a network test they're  doing it to stress test their servers so  while I'm really scared that this  mechanic will remain and that invading  multiple opponents will become the norm  I want to believe that it's just a  feature of the network test so let's  hope for that players did adapt to this  system in the tests it was really common  to invade a Fight Club where you fought  one enemy at a time in a civil manner  while this panel of judges watched on  from above the dried finger item was  really useful after every invasion to  reset the invasion timer and as a result  constant PvP ensued if you're a red or  white phantom you appear in another  world with roughly 70% of your health  you also invade with half of your  remaining estes both reds and whites can  use Estus flasks to heal both health and  mana and it's interesting most fights in  Dark Souls 3 that were long and  drawn-out revolved around the efficient  use of these flasks because killing  other players actually rewarded you with  Estus so that you could continue  fighting killing an invader as a host  gives you three estes killing an invader  as a white phantom gives you one estes  and killing a white as an invader gives  you two estes so this does a lot to  encourage fights between the host and  the invading player encouraging the host  to ant down the invader if they're low  on health or mana and encouraging the  invading phantom to hunt down the white  phantoms until they can take out the  hosts alone it meant that invasions  lasted a long time and were fuelled by  constant battle take the two white  phantoms in this clip for example they  were kind enough to die so that I could  keep my health topped up and it let me  keep up that pressure until the host was  left alone and I still had all the estus  in the world speaking of healing  it's nearly instant now and almost twice  as fast to take effect as in previous  souls games however in Dark Souls 3  after your health comes back your  character stands still for a really long  time  meaning that yeah it's really easy to  get their health back if you need it but  it's also very easy to get hit after you  heal look at this guy he got hit way too  many times and he used his Estes really  inefficiently and I think he's just  realizing that I killed both of his  phantoms he ended up getting away from  me in the end and at first I was really  annoyed that he got away but now in  retrospect it's kind of funny because  now he has to fight the boss with no  phantoms and no health and no Estus so I  win I'm pretty happy with the way  healing is right now because it's  possible to defeat your opponent both by  overpowering them when they have estes  and even by backstabbing them if they  use their estes badly it's good to have  both options i think speaking of which  let's talk about back steps they're  usually a pretty controversial topic  everyone remembers the lag back stabs of  Dark Souls 1 everyone remembers missing  a backstab in Dark Souls 2 because you  had to punch them first  and well no one really remembers  backstabs in blood-borne PvP do they in  Dark Souls 3  I think they've finally figured out the  perfect backstab system in Dark Souls 3  the animation for back stabs is similar  to in Dark Souls 1 where it's quite  instant but it is also possible to whiff  a backstab and miss like in Dark Souls 2  if you're not positioned properly  the whiff animation has you perform the  backstab animation but without locking  your opponent into place see this clip  my backstab attempt still hits the enemy  as if it were a regular attack and he  doesn't get locked into place and back  stabs are a good mechanic they do  deserve to exist because there needs to  be something to punish players who leave  themselves exposed for example if your  opponent spans Perry you deserve to be  able to backstab them to counter it if  your opponent heals too close to you you  deserve to be able to punish them for  that  unlike in blood-borne Dark Souls 3s  backstabs allow you to do these things  but they aren't completely unfair like  in Dark Souls 1 where players could take  advantage of the bad netcode no longer  can you do running back steps no longer  can you really fish for back stabs and I  feel like I deserved all of the back  stabs I got which is a pretty good thing  in previous games simply by virtue of  back stabs existing certain strengths  weapons were just bad because it was  easier to backstab punish a slow weapon  well not this time  because slow weapons actually have  really really good tracking check out  these clips where I was using the great  sword my character can pivot along with  my target while I'm carrying out the  attack which is a huge buff to strength  weapons this increased control is a big  deal because strength weapons have  traditionally been really bad in PvP  because it's easy to punish them so I  reckon they're going to be more viable  this time around before the network  tests  I thought the charge attacks were going  to be really bad charge attacks are  charged up by holding down r2 and you  can release it for massive damage but  charge attacks really do benefit from  the improved tracking some weapons like  the battle axe even had charge attacks  that would carry your character far  forward and these attacks honed in to  whatever you were targeting so  they could close the gap in PvP and they  were a lot easier to aim than I thought  they would be moving on if you break an  opponent's guard you can perform a guard  break repost similar to in Dark Souls 2  you can do this by kicking them with  their shield up  you can deplete their stamina while they  have their shield up or you can use the  guard break attack for the short-sword  weapon art r1 I think it's really  important that players get significantly  punished for bad stamina management or  for just leaving their shield up when it  doesn't need to be this mechanic does  really help the depth of the PvP and  that short sword might just be my  favorite weapon so far it has a really  simple move set and it's weapon arts  give the weapon a depth and a  versatility that it's never really had  before and I was honestly surprised at  how much depth weapon arts added if you  don't know weapon arts are activated  with l2 and they give you access to new  powerful moves that consume mana souls  games have always revolved around the  weapon that you choose and I do think  that webinar to give that weapon more  character than ever before let's talk  poise the rule in the network test was  pretty much if you get hit by one attack  it's very likely that you'll get hit by  a follow-up attack before you can roll  away while most attacks will stagger  your character once there are certain  attacks that grant your character hyper  armor preventing you from getting  staggered the great sword for example  seems to have hyper armor during some of  its swings are particularly the webinar  which certainly makes you able to tank  any hit but this poise  it's definitely subject to change I mean  everything in the network test is  subject to change but we do know for a  fact that equipment load hasn't been  taken into account in this demo as every  character you played regardless of  whether it had heavy plate armor or  light cloth it was able to roll around  without any drawbacks to using heavy  armor so it is possible that armor and  poise simply wasn't a factor in this  test so maybe it's still being worked on  there's a long time till this game comes  out remember another interesting thing  is that poise can actually be granted to  you when you cast miracles guys this  makes me so excited for miracle based  characters and also magic based  characters because both miracles and  magic have  benefits that are granted to you when  you press l2 with a catalyst or a  talisman  so with miracles when you press l2  instead of l1 to cast your spell your  character is granted this yellow glow  and a ton of poise allowing you to cast  spells in your enemies face without fear  of being staggered now this does come at  the cost of a little bit more manner but  it's a very powerful tool with this you  could tank an enemy's melee hits while  casting lightning spear to do even more  damage or cast heal when you're in the  midst of battle with a bit more  confidence if more melee based miracles  are introduced  I expect the Paladin archetype to be a  really good force to be reckoned with in  regards to sorcery if you press l2 with  a catalyst equipped it grants a damaged  buff to all of your spell's for the next  10 seconds so I was really excited when  I played the academy assassin class I  found it to be the most versatile in the  network test soul greatsword was  fantastic for cutting down a group of  phantoms and switching between soul  arrow and sold art made it really easy  to get in some hits from a distance and  just chipping away at phantom health  which is really useful all of these  tools are really really useful when  you're fighting a group of enemies  because it gives you versatility which  is the most important thing sometimes  you have to attack from a distance when  they're engaging with other enemies and  having that control over the battle was  great I mean I didn't really like magic  in previous souls games in PvP but I  have high hopes that it's going to be  good in Dark Souls 3 there was a lot of  information to get across what do you  guys think of it all I'm looking forward  to reading your comments down below  personally I am cautiously optimistic I  really want this game to be a game that  I can PvP and for the next year or so  because blood-borne just didn't offer  that for me here's hoping they get it  right I sincerely hope that they do  the next video will be on PvE highlights  from the network test so if you weren't  lucky enough to get in let me run you  through it subscribe so you don't miss  it  and I'll see you when the next one guys  looking forward to covering this a bit  more Cheers

